  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Post- \nTropical Cyclone Josephine well off the coast, resulting in \nwidespread minor coastal flooding continuing for back bay areas \nin Ocean County, NJ and Sussex County, DE.\n\nPost-Tropical Cyclone Josephine has pulled away from the region\nas it's now over the Canadian Maritimes. However elevated water\nlevels persist, mainly in the back bay areas of Ocean County, \nNJ and Sussex County, Delaware. As a result we expect another \nround of minor coastal flooding this evening with the high tide \ncycle. Along the oceanfront areas of these counties as well as \nour other counties along the Atlantic coast and Delware Bay, any\ntidal flooding this evening looks to be very spotty in nature. \nThe trend is for water levels to be gradually diminishing \nhowever it's possible coastal flooding for could even linger \nthrough Saturday for the back bays. The Coastal Flood Advisory \ncurrently in effect runs through midnight tonight but we will \nevaluate later this evening whether it needs to be extended. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for \nthe Maryland eastern shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freeze impacts likely tonight and \nSaturday night where the growing season continues. \n\nA Freeze Warning was issued for areas north and west of the \nI-95 corridor from midnight until 9AM Saturday where the growing\nseason remains active. For the I-95 corridor and areas south \nand east, a Frost Advisory was issued from midnight until 9AM \nSaturday for all other zones, excluding Sussex County in \nDelaware and the barrier islands in New Jersey.\n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast tonight, a\nstrong mid-level ridge in the Plains to Midwest shifts eastward\nthrough the weekend. This will support strong surface high \npressure shifting eastward into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic \nthrough the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead, forecast \nsoundings show surface winds decoupling. This will set the stage\nfor efficient radiational cooling both tonight and Saturday \nnight. The forecast lows are in the 30s for much of the area \n(with the only exception along the immediate coastal strip) with\na favorable setup for frost and freeze. Some guidance does show\nlight upper level stratus developing late overnight with the \nbest chance for cloud coverage further north and east, but \nconfidence remains low. Any light cloud coverage would be the \nonly limiting reagent to the efficient radiational cooling and \ncould slow cooling down by a few degrees at best, but it is not \nexpected to slam the brakes hard on the efficient radiational \ncooling setup overnight.\n\nAn additional round of frost/freeze impacts is possible \nSaturday night through Sunday morning for wherever the growing \nseason continues.",
